1. `describeCheckout` — `option_bag_junk_drawer`

Split the six-field bag into cohesive named inputs and destructure them:

```ts
interface CheckoutSelection {
  plan: string;
  currency: string;
}

interface CheckoutContext {
  role: string;
  region: string;
}

interface CheckoutState {
  status: string;
  shouldRetry: boolean;
}

export function describeCheckout(
  selection: CheckoutSelection,
  context: CheckoutContext,
  state: CheckoutState,
): string {
  const { plan, currency } = selection;
  const { role, region } = context;
  const { status, shouldRetry } = state;

  return [plan, role, region, currency, status, String(shouldRetry)].join(":");
}
```

This addresses `option_bag_junk_drawer` by exposing which concepts the function consumes instead of presenting one generic container. Today, an agent may add unrelated checkout settings to `CheckoutOptions`, confuse `retry` with a retry count, or pass a larger object whose incidental fields silently become part of this function’s contract.

2. `resolvePaymentResult` — `return_shape_roulette`

Declare a discriminated result union and make each variant explicit:

```ts
type PaymentResult =
  | {
      kind: "error";
      code: string;
      message: string;
    }
  | {
      kind: "redirect";
      url: string;
      permanent: boolean;
    }
  | {
      kind: "success";
      value: string;
      cached: boolean;
    };

export function resolvePaymentResult(/* input */): PaymentResult {
  if (input.error) {
    return {
      kind: "error",
      code: input.code,
      message: input.message,
    };
  }

  if (input.redirect) {
    return {
      kind: "redirect",
      url: input.url,
      permanent: false,
    };
  }

  return {
    kind: "success",
    value: input.value,
    cached: false,
  };
}
```

Ideally, make the input a discriminated union too, so `error` and `redirect` cannot both be true and branch-specific values are required.

This directly resolves `return_shape_roulette`: the current three shapes have as little as 0% key overlap. An agent could assume every result has `ok`, access `url` without narrowing, add a fourth incompatible shape, or overlook that `error` silently wins when both flags are true. A `kind` discriminator enables exhaustive handling and makes extensions compiler-visible.

3. `canRunBilling` — `negative_flag_maze`

Replace negative flags with positive state and return the predicate directly:

```ts
export function canRunBilling(
  isBillingEnabled: boolean,
  shouldRetry: boolean,
): boolean {
  return isBillingEnabled && shouldRetry;
}
```

At existing call sites, convert the old values explicitly:

```ts
canRunBilling(!disableBilling, !skipRetry);
```

A named object would be even clearer if the function is likely to gain more conditions:

```ts
canRunBilling({
  isBillingEnabled: !disableBilling,
  shouldRetry: !skipRetry,
});
```

This addresses `negative_flag_maze` by removing `!disableBilling && !skipRetry`. Today, an agent adding another exception could easily invert only one flag, confuse `skipRetry` with permission to run without retrying, or produce a hard-to-review expression with three or more negations.

Tests should lock down the serialized field order, exhaustively cover all payment-result variants, and use a four-case truth table for the billing predicate.
